    <h1>🐱 10 Games Like Sudoku You Can Play Free Online</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">Love Sudoku but want something fresh? These 10 logic puzzle games use the same part of your brain — spatial reasoning, deduction, and pattern recognition — but with different mechanics and themes.</p>

    <div class="game-card" style="border:2px solid #E8A888;">
      <h2>1. GridPaw (Akari / Light Up) <span class="tag tag-pick">⭐ Top Pick</span><span class="tag tag-free">Free</span><span class="tag tag-online">Online</span></h2>
      <p><strong>Why it's like Sudoku:</strong> Grid-based deduction with numbered clues. You place glowing cats on a grid, and each cat illuminates its row and column until blocked by a wall. Numbered black cells tell you exactly how many adjacent cells need cats.</p>
      <p><strong>Why it's different:</strong> Instead of filling numbers 1-9, you're thinking about <em>light propagation</em> — lines of sight, shadows, and spatial coverage. No math at all. The cozy cat theme makes it feel relaxing rather than intense.</p>
      <p><strong>Best for:</strong> Sudoku lovers who want spatial reasoning instead of number logic. Perfect for a chill brain workout.</p>
      <a class="play-link" href="/">▶ Play GridPaw Free</a>
    </div>

    <div class="game-card">
      <h2>2. Nonograms (Picross) <span class="tag tag-free">Free</span><span class="tag tag-online">Online</span></h2>
      <p><strong>Why it's like Sudoku:</strong> Grid-based, uses numbered clues, requires pure logic. Each row and column has numbers that tell you how many cells to fill in — and the filled cells form a picture.</p>
      <p><strong>Why it's different:</strong> The clues are about <em>consecutive groups</em> of filled cells, not individual cell values. The reward is a hidden picture when you finish.</p>
      <p><strong>Best for:</strong> People who like Sudoku's structure but want a visual payoff at the end.</p>
      <a class="external-link" href="https://nonograms.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Play Nonograms online →</a>
    </div>

    <div class="game-card">
      <h2>3. Kakuro <span class="tag tag-free">Free</span><span class="tag tag-online">Online</span></h2>
      <p><strong>Why it's like Sudoku:</strong> Grid-based number puzzle with unique-solution constraint. Each "word" (row/column segment) must sum to a target number using digits 1-9 with no repeats.</p>
      <p><strong>Why it's different:</strong> Combines Sudoku's no-repeat rule with arithmetic. You need both number logic <em>and</em> addition skills.</p>
      <p><strong>Best for:</strong> Sudoku fans who want to add math to the mix.</p>
      <a class="external-link" href="https://www.kakuros.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Play Kakuro online →</a>
    </div>

    <div class="game-card">
      <h2>4. Hitori <span class="tag tag-free">Free</span><span class="tag tag-online">Online</span></h2>
      <p><strong>Why it's like Sudoku:</strong> Grid of numbers with no-repeat constraints. You eliminate numbers so that no number appears twice in any row or column — and all remaining cells are connected.</p>
      <p><strong>Why it's different:</strong> You start with a <em>full</em> grid and remove numbers, rather than filling an empty one. It's Sudoku in reverse.</p>
      <p><strong>Best for:</strong> Sudoku players who want a "subtraction" puzzle instead of an "addition" one.</p>
      <a class="external-link" href="https://www.puzzle-hitori.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Play Hitori online →</a>
    </div>

    <div class="game-card">
      <h2>5. Fillomino <span class="tag tag-free">Free</span><span class="tag tag-online">Online</span></h2>
      <p><strong>Why it's like Sudoku:</strong> Grid-based number placement with adjacency constraints. You fill a grid so that each group of identical numbers has exactly that many cells.</p>
      <p><strong>Why it's different:</strong> Groups can be any shape (not just rows/columns), and you decide both the numbers and the group boundaries.</p>
      <p><strong>Best for:</strong> Sudoku players who want more freedom in how they partition the grid.</p>
      <a class="external-link" href="https://www.puzzle-fillomino.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Play Fillomino online →</a>
    </div>

    <div class="game-card">
      <h2>6. KenKen <span class="tag tag-free">Free</span><span class="tag tag-online">Online</span></h2>
      <p><strong>Why it's like Sudoku:</strong> Grid with no-repeat rows/columns. Each "cage" (outlined group) has a target number and operation (+, −, ×, ÷) that the cells must satisfy.</p>
      <p><strong>Why it's different:</strong> Adds arithmetic operations to the Sudoku framework. More varied than plain number placement.</p>
      <p><strong>Best for:</strong> Sudoku fans who enjoy mental math alongside logic.</p>
      <a class="external-link" href="https://www.kenkenpuzzle.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Play KenKen online →</a>
    </div>

    <div class="game-card">
      <h2>7. Hashiwokakero (Bridges) <span class="tag tag-free">Free</span><span class="tag tag-online">Online</span></h2>
      <p><strong>Why it's like Sudoku:</strong> Constraint satisfaction with numbered clues. Each island (numbered circle) must have exactly that many bridges connecting to neighboring islands.</p>
      <p><strong>Why it's different:</strong> It's a <em>graph</em> puzzle, not a grid-fill puzzle. You're building connections, not placing values.</p>
      <p><strong>Best for:</strong> Sudoku players who want a topological challenge.</p>
      <a class="external-link" href="https://www.puzzle-bridges.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Play Bridges online →</a>
    </div>

    <div class="game-card">
      <h2>8. Slitherlink <span class="tag tag-free">Free</span><span class="tag tag-online">Online</span></h2>
      <p><strong>Why it's like Sudoku:</strong> Grid-based deduction with numbered clues. Each number tells you how many edges of that cell are part of a single closed loop.</p>
      <p><strong>Why it's different:</strong> You're drawing a loop, not filling cells. The visual feedback is completely different — and deeply satisfying when the loop closes.</p>
      <p><strong>Best for:</strong> Sudoku players who want a more visual, spatial challenge.</p>
      <a class="external-link" href="https://www.puzzle-loop.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Play Slitherlink online →</a>
    </div>

    <div class="game-card">
      <h2>9. Futoshiki <span class="tag tag-free">Free</span><span class="tag tag-online">Online</span></h2>
      <p><strong>Why it's like Sudoku:</strong> Grid with no-repeat rows/columns. Additional inequality signs between cells add constraints (e.g., this cell must be greater than its neighbor).</p>
      <p><strong>Why it's different:</strong> Uses <em>relative comparisons</em> instead of absolute counts. You reason about "greater than" and "less than" rather than exact numbers.</p>
      <p><strong>Best for:</strong> Sudoku fans who want ordering logic on top of placement logic.</p>
      <a class="external-link" href="https://www.futoshiki.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Play Futoshiki online →</a>
    </div>

    <div class="game-card">
      <h2>10. Masyu (Pearl Puzzle) <span class="tag tag-free">Free</span><span class="tag tag-online">Online</span></h2>
      <p><strong>Why it's like Sudoku:</strong> Grid-based deduction with two types of clues (black and white pearls). You draw a single loop that passes through all pearls, turning at black pearls and going straight at white ones.</p>
      <p><strong>Why it's different:</strong> Like Slitherlink, it's a loop puzzle — but the clue types are simpler and the logic is more about path-finding.</p>
      <p><strong>Best for:</strong> Sudoku players who want a minimalist spatial puzzle.</p>
      <a class="external-link" href="https://www.puzzle-masyu.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Play Masyu online →</a>
    </div>

